WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.330
"We’ll all fly away together…&nbsp;
one last time."

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:10.200
The completion of James Gunn’s Guardians trilogy has some&nbsp;
truly heartbreaking moments. But one death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.460
was seriously not who we were expecting.&nbsp;
Keep watching to find out who that was.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:19.200
We've known for a long while that the Grim Reaper&nbsp;
has been sharpening its scythe ahead of "Guardians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.240
of the Galaxy Vol. 3." Historically, James Gunn&nbsp;
has killed characters if the reason is good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.400
and has done so in both previous "Guardians"&nbsp;
movies — the original Groot in the first one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.900
and Yondu in "Guardians of the Galaxy&nbsp;
Vol. 2." What's more, Gunn dropped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.820
the huge "Vol. 3" bombshell about another&nbsp;
impending character death way back in 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:44.520
so fans have had plenty of time to think about the&nbsp;
"Guardians" characters who are most likely to die.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:50.340
Well, Gunn certainly wasn't lying. "Guardians&nbsp;
of the Galaxy Vol. 3" features plenty of death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:55.200
from the almost casual end of Sovereign&nbsp;
leader Ayesha to the latest civilization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.060
the High Evolutionary destroys. However, fans&nbsp;
who have been bracing themselves for the death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:05.400
of a central Guardian character can walk away&nbsp;
from the movie relieved (or disappointed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.480
depending on your outlook). Despite&nbsp;
multiple extremely close calls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:15.180
the heroes all survive the movie and even get&nbsp;
a happy ending to their various character arcs.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:18.060
That doesn't mean the movie doesn't&nbsp;
feature a particularly grueling death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:23.280
though — it's just that instead of killing off&nbsp;
a familiar name, "Vol. 3" opts to establish a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.080
new likable character for this purpose. In what&nbsp;
just might be the most emotional moment in a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.880
that's chock full of them, the High Evolutionary&nbsp;
guns Lylla down at the beginning of Rocket's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:38.460
escape plan. It's a pivotal moment in both the&nbsp;
movie and Rocket's life, and though we've only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:44.040
known Lylla for a few scenes, her death and its&nbsp;
impact on Rocket is nothing short of devastating.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:48.420
It can be difficult to market a movie in&nbsp;
a way that attracts potential viewers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.800
yet doesn't give away too much of the&nbsp;
plot and the big beats. Because of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.360
promotional material has to straddle&nbsp;
a fine line, and the end result isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.800
always successful — in fact, there are many&nbsp;
trailers that gave away too much of the movie.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:07.080
"Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 3," on the other&nbsp;
hand, takes a different approach. While the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:11.280
itself enjoys baiting the viewer with numerous&nbsp;
close calls when it comes to character deaths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:16.740
the trailers deceive the viewer in the opposite&nbsp;
way when it comes to Lylla. Marvel fans might go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.880
into the movie with the knowledge that Lylla&nbsp;
and Rocket have quite a happy storyline in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.160
the comics — definitely happier than what they&nbsp;
get in the movie. As such, when the promotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:31.260
materials see the adorable otter embracing Rocket&nbsp;
or laughing on the floor, it's all too easy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:35.820
take these fleeting moments of happiness as a&nbsp;
sign of a happy reunion for current-day Rocket.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:41.160
In reality, however, the movie takes a stark turn&nbsp;
for the worse at the moment that happy hug from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:46.980
the trailers happens. Barely a second after this&nbsp;
familiar moment, the High Evolutionary ruthlessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:52.200
shoots Lylla in the back and proceeds to mock&nbsp;
Rocket. As if things weren't already bad enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:57.240
Floor and Teefs perish in the chaos that ensues&nbsp;
from the shooting and Rocket's attack on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:00.960
High Evolutionary, leaving the enhanced&nbsp;
raccoon well and truly alone in the world.&nbsp;

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:07.020
Sure, Lylla is a relatively minor character in&nbsp;
the grand scheme of the Marvel Cinematic Universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:11.400
However, her influence on Rocket's life,&nbsp;
the shocking circumstances of her death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.940
and the immediate, face-ripping&nbsp;
devastation Rocket unleashes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.900
retaliation add up to one of the most&nbsp;
impactful death scenes in the franchise.

